// The in-app notification inbox.
//
// This service owns the table outright: it is the only writer, and the
// user-facing list/read/count endpoints read it directly. user_service used to
// own these rows, but a notifications table that lives in the accounts service
// cannot be joined, counted or paginated without a network hop, so ownership
// moved here.
//
// The column types are deliberate rather than whatever Prisma would pick by
// default, because this table is being adopted from Django's ORM:
//
//   id / read_at / created_at  Django's DateTimeField is `timestamptz`, and
//                              timestamptz(6) keeps the microsecond precision
//                              that auto_now_add stored. Prisma's default
//                              `timestamp(3)` would silently round every
//                              created_at to milliseconds, which both reorders
//                              rows created in the same millisecond and changes
//                              what the client is told about when it happened.
//   user_id                    bigint in Django. Prisma's `Int` would have made
//                              it int4, which silently caps user ids at
//                              2^31-1; `@db.BigInt` keeps the 64-bit column
//                              while staying a plain JS number, so the id needs
//                              no bigint handling on its way into JSON.
//   type / title               varchar, as declared in the Django model.
//   metadata                   jsonb, matching Django's JSONField.
//
// There is no relation to a user: the id belongs to user_service's own table in
// a different database, so `userId` is a plain column. That is also what makes
// the composite index below usable — Postgres can seek it without a join.
model Notification {
  id        String    @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  // bigint, matching Django's BigAutoField user id. Prisma surfaces a BigInt
  // field as a JS bigint, so the repository takes a plain number and converts
  // once at the query boundary rather than letting `number` leak into types
  // that would then need bigint-aware JSON handling.
  userId    BigInt    @map("user_id")
  type      String    @db.VarChar(64)
  title     String    @db.VarChar(255)
  body      String    @default("")
  metadata  Json      @default("{}")
  readAt    DateTime? @map("read_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  // dedupe_key: the producer's stable identity for the logical event, derived
  // from the domain object that caused it (a VerificationToken id, for example)
  // rather than from the notification itself. Null for the many notifications
  // that are not worth deduplicating.
  //
  // Postgres treats NULLs as distinct in a btree unique index, so this plain
  // UNIQUE already permits any number of rows with a NULL dedupe_key — a
  // partial index would be smaller but is not representable in the schema.
  //
  // Two retry paths converge on this: the producer's HTTP client retrying after
  // a timeout that followed a committed insert, and BullMQ re-running a job.
  // Without it, both surface to the user as a duplicate inbox row.
  dedupeKey String? @map("dedupe_key") @db.VarChar(255)

  // archived_at: set when the user dismisses a notification. Distinct from
  // read_at on purpose — read means "seen", archived means "no longer in the
  // inbox" — so dismissing does not have to overwrite when it was read, and the
  // retention job can keep unread rows while pruning dismissed ones.
  archivedAt DateTime? @map("archived_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  // idx_user_archived_created: the default inbox list. Leading with user_id and
  // archived_at narrows the query to one user's live rows instead of scanning the
  // table, and created_at/id DESC match the newest-first order.
  //
  // It does not eliminate the sort: Postgres treats `archived_at IS NULL` as a
  // filter here rather than an equality scan key, so it bitmap-scans the user's
  // rows and sorts that slice in memory. Measured on a 30k-row table that sort is
  // tens of microseconds over tens of rows. Eliminating it would need partial
  // indexes per list view, which is not worth three extra indexes on the write
  // path for an inbox that is read a few times per session.
  @@index([userId, archivedAt, createdAt(sort: Desc), id(sort: Desc)], map: "idx_user_archived_created")
  // idx_user_read_archived_created: the unread list and the unread count, which are
  // user_id + read_at IS NULL + archived_at IS NULL. Same narrowing, and because
  // unread is a small subset the scan is correspondingly tighter.
  @@index([userId, readAt, archivedAt, createdAt(sort: Desc), id(sort: Desc)], map: "idx_user_read_archived_created")
  // idx_read_created / idx_archived_created: the retention sweep, which prunes on
  // read_at IS NOT NULL OR archived_at IS NOT NULL -- either endpoint means the user
  // has dealt with the row. Each index narrows its own branch and carries
  // created_at for the cutoff range, and Postgres bitmap-ORs the two.
  @@index([readAt, createdAt], map: "idx_read_created")
  @@index([archivedAt, createdAt], map: "idx_archived_created")
  @@index([type], map: "idx_notification_type")
  // Expressed here rather than as a hand-written partial index so Prisma can
  // manage it. The cost is that rows with a NULL dedupe_key are indexed too; a
  // partial index would be smaller, but it is not representable in the schema and
  // would read as drift on the next migrate diff.
  @@unique([userId, dedupeKey], map: "uq_notifications_user_dedupe")
  @@map("notifications")
}
